[
    {
        "content": "<p>I have a bit of a short proof segment, that is solved by the following snippet</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">refine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨?</span><span class=\"n\">obvious</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">in_orbit</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">obvious</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">use</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">in_orbit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a_in_class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I would really like to replace this with <code>exact ⟨use a, a_in_class a⟩</code>, but I cannot use <code>use</code> inside the exact. is there some term-level construct that is able to do this? <code>exact ⟨(by use a), a_in_class a⟩</code> works but it also does not look good</p>",
        "id": 591014477,
        "sender_full_name": "incertia",
        "timestamp": 1777247299
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>in particular, it is somewhat annoying that <code>(by use a)</code> must be in parens as otherwise, it interprets the rest of the line as part of the same <code>by</code> block</p>",
        "id": 591014546,
        "sender_full_name": "incertia",
        "timestamp": 1777247380
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>An <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a> would be a bit easier to answer, but usually that's <code>exact ⟨a, a_in_class a⟩</code></p>",
        "id": 591014585,
        "sender_full_name": "Julian Berman",
        "timestamp": 1777247440
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>exact &lt;a, ...&gt; does not work, but the full example is</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unions_into_set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⋃₀</span><span class=\"n\">equiv_classes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">univ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rewrite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ext_iff</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">refine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨?</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- a ∈ ⋃₀ equiv_classes e → a ∈ Set.univ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- a ∈ Set.univ → a ∈ ⋃₀ equiv_classes e</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">rewrite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_sUnion</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">use</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">setOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">unfold</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">equiv_classes</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">rewrite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_setOf_eq</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- again refine into an obvious case and a reduction to a ∈ setOf (f a)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--refine ⟨?obvious, ?in_orbit⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--case obvious =&gt; use a</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--case in_orbit =&gt; exact a_in_class a</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">use</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a_in_class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>where e : Equivalence f and equiv_classes is just {setOf (f a) : a in A}. the first term of obvious is of type <code>∃ a_1 ∈ Set.univ, setOf (f a_1) = setOf (f a)</code>, which is probably why exact &lt;a, ...&gt; is insufficient</p>",
        "id": 591014884,
        "sender_full_name": "incertia",
        "timestamp": 1777247799
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>i guess maybe in this case <code>use</code> also performs additional simplifications, which makes it harder to directly write <code>use &lt;a, ...&gt;</code> and we should rely on the <code>use</code> tactic to perform the additional plumbing</p>",
        "id": 591014992,
        "sender_full_name": "incertia",
        "timestamp": 1777247901
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>did you read <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a>? (that's a link)</p>\n<p>this is not a mwe since when I click the link to take me to the web editor it gives me <code>unexpected token 'have'; expected command</code></p>",
        "id": 591015016,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1777247927
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Since <code>by use</code> works, you're probably missing some <code>rfl</code>s or <code>assumption</code>s.<br>\nYou can try <code>exact ⟨⟨a, rfl⟩, a_in_class a⟩</code>, and if that fails replace the <code>rfl</code> with an assumption, or maybe <code>rfl, rfl</code>, etc.<br>\nYou can also do <code>by show_term use a</code> to see what <code>use</code> did exactly, and copy that</p>",
        "id": 591015394,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1777248186
    }
]